"Share a link, get a swarm. Nuée turns the laptops and phones of a group that trusts each other\ninto a private, short-lived compute and file cluster. No server of ours, no account, no payment."
"```sh\nbrew install maxgfr/tap/nuee\nnuee run demo:mandelbrot\n# open the printed link (or scan the QR code) on a phone: the tab starts computing\n```"
"The web page is https://maxgfr.github.io/nuee/. It can join a swarm, run one, share a file or\nopen any Nuée link, with nothing to install."
"- **Verified CPU jobs in WebAssembly.** A coordinator (the CLI or the web page) splits a job into\n  tasks; CLI workers (`nuee join`) and browser tabs run them. The same module gives the same\n  output bytes in wasmtime, Chromium, Firefox and Safari (WebKit); the end-to-end tests check\n  this on a golden corpus. Results are checked by recomputation (see\n  [Verification](#verification)).\n- **Demos.** `demo:mandelbrot` (a fractal, written out as an image) and `demo:skycmd`\n  (closed-loop evaluation of the skycmd s3-pico flight model, downloaded and checked against\n  its BLAKE3 hash).\n- **Your own jobs.** A job TOML file, or any `.wasm` module that follows the task ABI plus a\n  directory of inputs (one file per task).\n- **Files.** `nuee share` encrypts files or folders with [age](https://age-encryption.org)\n  before they leave the machine and prints a link; `nuee get` or the web page downloads,\n  decrypts and checks them against BLAKE3. `nuee pin` keeps a ciphertext-only copy and serves\n  it, so the original sender can go offline.\n- **Your own relay.** `nuee relay` runs an iroh relay with Let's Encrypt, an optional bearer\n  token, a connection cap and a per-client rate limit.\n- **Interrupted runs resume.** Outputs already in the output directory are kept; run the same\n  command again."
"Not in this build yet: `train`, `sweep`, `embed`, `expose`, `connect` and `site` exist as verbs\nbut exit with \"not available in this build\". GPU tasks, `exec`, durable storage and WebRTC\nare not shipped either."
"All verbs print human text on stderr. `--json` prints NDJSON events on stdout instead."
"```console\n$ nuee run demo:mandelbrot\nhttps://maxgfr.github.io/nuee/#/J/AAQ...\nfingerprint 🚑 🍋 🚂 🌾 🚲 🧄\npeer joined: 5b1c…  (nuee-web/0.1.0)\nverified 212 · 3 peers · 38.5/s\ndone: 256 tasks, results in nuee-out/mandelbrot\nimage: nuee-out/mandelbrot/mandelbrot.ppm\n```"
"The link and a QR code appear only once the relay is reachable (10 seconds at most, otherwise\nexit code 3). Useful options: `--verify full|sample|none` (default `sample`), `--sample 0.10`,\n`--canary 0.03`, `--approve` (admit each peer by hand), `--wait-peers N`, `--local-slots N`,\n`--deadline 30s`, `--out DIR`, `--no-resume`, `--qr-svg FILE`."
"```sh\nnuee run my-task.wasm --inputs inputs/   # one file per task, sorted by name\n```"
"```console\n$ nuee join -          # paste the link on stdin, it stays out of your shell history\ncoordinator fingerprint 🚑 🍋 🚂 🌾 🚲 🧄  (compare with the coordinator's screen)\njoined with 5 slots\njob 1: mandelbrot\ntask 17 done in 41 ms (1 so far)\nleft the swarm after 87 tasks\n```"
"Compare the six emoji with the coordinator's screen before leaving it running. `--slots N` sets\nhow many tasks run at once (default: half the cores)."
"```console\n$ nuee share report.pdf photos/\nhttps://maxgfr.github.io/nuee/#/F/AAQ...\nserving 48213990 bytes (encrypted) until Ctrl-C"
"$ nuee get -o downloads/ -     # link on stdin\n```"
"`nuee get` refuses to overwrite existing files unless given `--force`, and refuses archive\nentries that would land outside the output directory."
"```sh\nnuee pin -            # keep a ciphertext copy of a file or pin link\nnuee pin --list\nnuee pin --rm <HASH>\nnuee serve            # serve every pinned item until Ctrl-C\n```"
"```sh\nnuee relay --hostname relay.example.org --acme-contact you@example.org --token-file token.txt\nnuee relay --dev --bind 127.0.0.1:3340      # plain HTTP, for local use and tests\nnuee --relay https://relay.example.org run demo:mandelbrot\n```"
"```console\n$ nuee selftest       # offline: no key, no network, no files\n✓ wasm golden: 3 golden outputs match\n✓ links: job, file and pin links round-trip\n✓ manifest: manifest signatures and admission proofs hold\n✓ encryption: age encryption round-trips and detects tampering\n✓ swarm: in-memory swarm finished 6 tasks\nselftest ok"
"$ nuee doctor         # identity, data directory, relay, wasm, sandbox, clock\n$ nuee id             # node id and its fingerprint\n$ nuee completions zsh\n```"
"Global options: `--relay URL`, `--relay-only` (no direct connections), `--web-base URL`,\n`--config-dir DIR`, `--data-dir DIR`, `--ephemeral` (throwaway identity, writes nothing),\n`-v`, `-q`, `--json`."
"- **The link is the capability.** A job link carries the coordinator's public key, its relay\n  and a 16-byte secret. Anyone holding the link can join; treat it like a password. Links can\n  be read from stdin (`-`) so they do not end up in shell history.\n- **Admission** is an HMAC-SHA256 proof over both peers' keys, compared in constant time.\n  Connections are QUIC with mutual TLS 1.3 through iroh, end to end.\n- **Fingerprints.** Six emoji (48 bits) derived only from the coordinator's key. Workers show\n  them so people can compare with the coordinator's screen.\n- **Signed manifests and results.** The coordinator signs each job manifest; workers sign each\n  result. Signatures use domain separation.\n- **Verification by recomputation.** In `sample` mode the coordinator reruns a share of the\n  results itself and mixes in known-answer canary tasks that look like any other task. In\n  `full` mode every task runs twice. A mismatch is settled by the coordinator's own run; only\n  then is the peer banned and all its unverified results redone.\n- **Worker-side caps.** Workers enforce their own limits on memory, module size, inputs,\n  outputs and time, whatever the manifest says. Modules must have zero imports and a declared\n  memory maximum; Wasm features outside a fixed list (relaxed SIMD, threads and others) are\n  refused.\n- **Files** are encrypted with age before leaving the machine and checked with BLAKE3 on\n  arrival. Pinners only ever hold ciphertext."
"Details: [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) (reporting, scope) and\n[docs/THREAT-MODEL.md](docs/THREAT-MODEL.md). The wire format is in\n[docs/PROTOCOL.md](docs/PROTOCOL.md)."
"- **Relay operators see browser IP addresses.** Traffic through a relay is end-to-end\n  encrypted, but the relay sees who connects. Other peers do not see a browser's IP.\n- **The n0 public relays are for development and hobby use.** For anything else, run your own\n  with `nuee relay`, or use n0's paid relays.\n- **Browser keys are ephemeral.** One person holding the link can open many tabs, so \"two\n  different peers agree\" proves nothing. Real assurance comes from the coordinator's own\n  recomputation, or from `--approve`.\n- **Mobile tabs stop in the background.** Keep the screen on while contributing.\n- **No Windows binary.** Windows machines join through the browser.\n- Nuée is for groups that trust each other enough to share a link. It is not an open market."
"| Project | What it is for | Difference with Nuée |\n|---|---|---|\n| Akash | A marketplace to rent cloud compute, paid in tokens | Nuée has no market, no payment, no provider to rent from |\n| Salad | Paid rental of consumer GPUs to companies | Nuée runs among people who know each other, unpaid |\n| Mesh LLM | Pooling machines to serve a large language model, built on iroh | Nuée runs verified batch jobs and shares files; it does not split a model across peers |\n| Buzz (Block) | A peer-to-peer application built on iroh | Nuée is a compute and file swarm joined from a link or a browser tab |\n| BOINC | Volunteer computing for research projects, run from a project server | Nuée needs no server: the coordinator is a laptop or a web page, joined from a link |"
"| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| 0 | ok |\n| 1 | error |\n| 2 | usage (bad arguments, truncated or wrong link) |\n| 3 | network or relay unreachable |\n| 4 | verification mismatch or banned |\n| 5 | admission refused |\n| 6 | integrity failure (hash or decryption) |\n| 7 | cancelled or interrupted |\n| 8 | swarm ended by the coordinator |"
"| Variable | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `NUEE_CONFIG_DIR` | identity key directory (default `~/.config/nuee`) |\n| `NUEE_DATA_DIR` | data directory (default `~/.local/share/nuee`) |\n| `NUEE_ONLINE_TIMEOUT_MS` | how long to wait for the relay (default 10000) |\n| `NO_COLOR` | disable colors |"
"This document describes the protocol as implemented in `crates/nuee-proto` (types, encoding,\nlinks, manifests, signatures) and `crates/nuee-net` (framing, admission). When this text and the\ncode disagree, the code is right and this file has a bug."
"All integers in postcard encodings are varints unless stated otherwise; all fixed-layout integers\noutside postcard (framing, signed messages, envelopes) have the byte order given explicitly."
"Every peer is an iroh endpoint. Its identity (`EndpointId`) is an ed25519 public key; connections\nare QUIC with TLS 1.3 where both sides authenticate with their key, so the remote identity of a\nconnection is known before the first byte of application data."
"| ALPN | Constant | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nuee/job/0` | `ALPN_JOB` | Swarm control: admission, jobs, leases, results, WebRTC signalling |\n| iroh-blobs ALPN | (iroh-blobs) | Modules, assets, outputs above 1 MiB, shared files. Only admitted peers; push disabled |\n| `nuee/pin/0` | `ALPN_PIN` | Replication orders |\n| `nuee/tcp/0` | `ALPN_TCP` | Exposed TCP service tunnel |\n| `nuee/http/0` | `ALPN_HTTP` | Console HTTP requests |"
"`PROTO_VERSION = 0` is carried in every `Hello` and every manifest. The trailing `/0` of each ALPN\nis the major version of that sub-protocol."
"Messages are serialized with [postcard](https://postcard.jamesmunk.ooo/) (serde). Enum variants\nare encoded as a varint index in declaration order; adding a variant at the end is compatible,\nreordering is not."
"1. rejects `bytes.len() > max` (`ProtoError::TooLarge`) before parsing;\n2. decodes with postcard;\n3. rejects any trailing byte (`ProtoError::Trailing`)."
"`decode` uses `max = MAX_MSG` (8 MiB). Console messages (`HttpRequest`, `HttpResponse`) use\n`MAX_HTTP_MSG = MAX_HTTP_BODY + 512 KiB`. `encode_with_limit` refuses to produce more than `max`\nbytes."
"Every variable-length field of a wire type uses one of:"
"| Type | Encoding | Cap check |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BoundedBytes<N>` | postcard byte string (varint length + bytes) | length > N rejected before copying |\n| `BoundedString<N>` | postcard string (varint length + UTF-8) | byte length > N rejected; invalid UTF-8 rejected |\n| `BoundedVec<T, N>` | postcard sequence (varint count + items) | count > N rejected before allocating; the pre-allocation is `min(count, N)` |"
"Nested unbounded collections (`Vec<Vec<_>>`) never appear in wire types."
"Fixed-size types: `Hash` = 32 raw bytes (BLAKE3), `EndpointId` = 32 bytes (must be a valid\ned25519 point), `Signature` = 64 bytes, `[u8; N]` = N raw bytes."
"On every stream of `nuee/job/0` (and the other nuee ALPNs), a message is one frame:"
"```\nu32 big-endian length || postcard payload (length bytes)\n```"
"`read_frame(recv, max)` reads the 4-byte header, rejects `length > max` before allocating the\nbuffer, then reads exactly `length` bytes. A stream that ends before the header is a clean close;\none that ends inside a frame is `Truncated`. The first frame on `nuee/job/0` (the `Hello`) is\ncapped at `MAX_HELLO = 64 KiB`; later frames at `MAX_MSG`."
"A link holder proves knowledge of the 16-byte link secret without sending it:"
"```\nproof = HMAC-SHA256(key = secret, label || client_id (32) || server_id (32))\n```"
"| Sub-protocol | Label |\n|---|---|\n| `nuee/job/0` | `nuee/hello/0` (`PROOF_HELLO`) |\n| `nuee/pin/0` | `nuee/pin-hello/0` (`PROOF_PIN`) |\n| `nuee/tcp/0` | `nuee/tcp-hello/0` (`PROOF_TCP`) |\n| `nuee/http/0` | `nuee/http-hello/0` (`PROOF_HTTP`) |"
"Binding both identities makes a proof useless on any other connection: a relay or a different\ncoordinator cannot replay it. The server recomputes the MAC and compares with\n`hmac::Mac::verify_slice` (constant time). Secrets come from the OS random source\n(`new_secret16`)."
"1. The worker opens one bidirectional stream and sends `Msg::Hello{version, proof, caps}`.\n2. The coordinator, under `HELLO_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000`, checks `version == PROTO_VERSION` and the\n   proof. At most `MAX_PENDING_UNAUTH = 64` handshakes run at once; more are closed at once.\n   Five wrong proofs from one `EndpointId` within 60 s refuse it for 60 s (`nuee-net::admit`).\n3. On success the peer is admitted to the blob store and the core state machine receives it. The\n   coordinator answers `Pending` (manual approval mode) or `Welcome{..}`; a refusal is\n   `Bye{Rejected}` or `Bye{Full}`.\n4. All later traffic flows on this same stream pair."
"QUIC application close codes (`nuee-net::driver::close_code`):"
"| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| 0 | done |\n| 1 | refused (bad proof, bad version, rejected) |\n| 2 | busy (too many handshakes, throttled) |\n| 3 | hello timeout |\n| 4 | protocol error |\n| 5 | banned |\n| 6 | swarm full |\n| 7 | shutdown |"
"## 4. Messages on `nuee/job/0` (`enum Msg`)"
"| # | Variant | Direction | Fields and caps |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | `Hello` | W→C | `version: u16`, `proof: [u8;32]`, `caps: Caps` |\n| 1 | `Pending` | C→W | none |\n| 2 | `Welcome` | C→W | `swarm: SwarmInfo`, `jobs: BoundedVec<SignedManifest,16>`, `webrtc: bool`, `stun: BoundedVec<BoundedString<256>,4>` |\n| 3 | `Job` | C→W | `SignedManifest` |\n| 4 | `Want` | W→C | `slots: u16` (n > 0 adds credit, 0 pauses) |\n| 5 | `Lease` | C→W | `Lease` (below) |\n| 6 | `Result` | W→C | `lease: u64`, `output: Payload`, `output_hash: Hash`, `duration_ms: u32`, `sig: Signature` |\n| 7 | `Fail` | W→C | `lease: u64`, `kind: FailKind`, `detail: BoundedString<256>` |\n| 8 | `RtcOffer` | both | `RtcSignal` |\n| 9 | `RtcAnswer` | both | `RtcSignal` |\n| 10 | `RtcIce` | both | `RtcSignal` |\n| 11 | `Cancel` | C→W | `lease: u64` |\n| 12 | `Bye` | both | `reason: ByeReason` |"
"- `Caps{agent: BoundedString<64>, browser: bool, cpu_slots: u16, gpu: Option<GpuCaps>,\n  mem_mb: u32, kinds: u32, cached: BoundedVec<Hash,64>, exec_allow:\n  BoundedVec<BoundedString<160>,16>, store_quota_mb: u64, relay: Option<BoundedString<256>>,\n  rtc: bool}`. `kinds` is a bitset, bit `i` = `TaskKind` with index `i`.\n- `GpuCaps{adapter: BoundedString<64>, max_buffer: u64, max_storage_binding: u64, f16: bool}`.\n- `SwarmInfo{name: BoundedString<64>, coordinator: EndpointId, mode: SwarmMode, file:\n  Option<FileInfo{hash, size: u64}>}`; `SwarmMode = Compute | Files | Service | Site`.\n- `Lease{lease: u64, job: u32, task: u32, kind: TaskKind, module: Option<Hash>, assets:\n  BoundedVec<Hash,16>, input: Payload, deadline_ms: u32}`.\n- `Payload = Inline(BoundedBytes<1 MiB>) | Blob{hash: Hash, size: u64}`. A blob is served by the\n  sender's iroh-blobs store to admitted peers.\n- `RtcSignal{peer: EndpointId, payload: BoundedString<16 KiB>}`. `peer` is the target when\n  sent, the source when relayed. The coordinator relays at most 64 signals per peer per minute\n  and only when WebRTC is enabled.\n- `TaskKind = Wasm | Wgsl | Embed | Llm | Exec | TrainWasm | TrainExec` (indices 0..6).\n- `FailKind = Trap | Oom | Timeout | StackOverflow | Invalid | Unsupported | Cancelled | Io`.\n- `ByeReason = Done | Banned | Shutdown | Rejected | Full`."
"This document lists what Nuée protects, against whom, how, and where in the code each\nmitigation lives. Wire details are in `docs/PROTOCOL.md`. Where a mitigation belongs to a\ncomponent that is still being built, the location is marked *(in progress)*; treat those rows\nas requirements, not guarantees, until the marker is gone."
"- **Contributor machines**: CPU, GPU, memory, disk, network and local files of everyone who\n  runs `nuee join` or opens a join link in a browser.\n- **Coordinator machine**: same, plus its identity key and the link secrets it issued.\n- **Result integrity**: the outputs a coordinator accepts and writes to disk.\n- **Shared content**: files and sites shared through `F`/`S` links, and the age keys that\n  decrypt them.\n- **Secrets**: endpoint secret keys, 16-byte link secrets, access tokens, relay tokens.\n- **Privacy**: IP addresses and who participates in which swarm.\n- **The web origin** `maxgfr.github.io`: whatever runs there runs with the authority of the app."
"| Actor | Has | Wants |\n|---|---|---|\n| Link holder | A valid link (bearer secret), a fresh endpoint key | Whatever the link grants; possibly more |\n| Malicious coordinator | A swarm, signed manifests, task modules, workers that trust its link | Code execution, data or resources on workers beyond the sandbox |\n| Malicious worker | Admission to a swarm; possibly many ephemeral identities | Make the coordinator accept wrong results, stall the swarm, reach other workers |\n| Relay operator | Runs an iroh relay used by peers | Read or alter traffic, learn who talks to whom |\n| Network attacker | On-path position, or none at all (the open internet) | Impersonate a coordinator, read traffic, exhaust resources, guess secrets |\n| Hosted content | HTML/JS shared as a site or served by an exposed service | Run script in the app origin, phish, steal secrets |"
"1. **Network → nuee-net.** Every byte from a peer is untrusted until the admission proof is\n   checked, and stays untrusted afterwards: admitted peers can still send garbage.\n2. **Manifest → worker.** A worker trusts that a manifest comes from the coordinator named in its\n   link (signature), not that the manifest is reasonable: local limits apply on top.\n3. **Task module → host.** WebAssembly modules run in wasmtime natively and in the browser's\n   engine on the web, with no imports at all.\n4. **`exec` job → worker OS.** Native code; crossed only when the worker opted in for that\n   exact repository and revision.\n5. **Shared content → browser.** Never rendered in the app origin.\n6. **Peer ↔ relay.** The relay forwards encrypted QUIC packets; it is not trusted with content."
"### 4.1 Hostile code on a contributor machine (malicious coordinator)"
"- Modules must have zero imports, no start function, exactly one 32-bit non-shared memory with a\n  declared maximum of at most 1 GiB, and the exact ABI exports. Module size is capped at 32 MiB.\n  *`nuee-proto::validate::validate_module`*, fuzzed by `fuzz/fuzz_targets/validate_module.rs`.\n- One shared list of allowed WebAssembly proposals (bulk-memory, multivalue, mutable-globals,\n  nontrapping-fptoint, reference-types, sign-ext, simd128); everything else, relaxed SIMD\n  included, is refused both by the validator and by the engine configuration.\n  *`nuee-proto::features::WASM_FEATURES`, applied in `nuee-exec::wasm` (engine `Config`).*\n- Runtime limits: fresh instance per lease, `ResourceLimiter` capping memory growth at the\n  smaller of the manifest and the worker's own limit, epoch interruption for deadlines, bounded\n  stack (`max_wasm_stack`), capped output size. *`nuee-exec::wasm`.*\n- Worker-side caps whatever the manifest says (`LocalLimits`: module, asset, memory, input,\n  output, concurrent leases). *`nuee-proto::limits::LocalLimits`, enforced in\n  `nuee-core::worker`.*\n- WGSL shaders validated by naga, bounded time. *`nuee-gpu` (in progress).*\n- Manifests are checked for consistency and limits before use. *`nuee-proto::manifest::Manifest::check`.*"
"Residual risk: a task inside its limits can still use all the CPU time and memory it was granted,\nand engine bugs (wasmtime, browser engines) are outside our control. Workers can pause or leave\nat any time."
"Mitigations *(in progress: `nuee-exec::native`, `nuee` CLI `join`)*:"
"- Disabled by default. A worker enables it with `--allow-exec repo@rev` (or an interactive TTY\n  confirmation) for an exact repository and full 40-hex commit, with the exact argv.\n- The manifest must carry a 40-character lowercase hex `rev`; checked in\n  *`nuee-proto::manifest::Manifest::check`* and the job file parser\n  *`nuee-proto::job_toml`*.\n- After checkout the commit is verified again; the environment is cleared; the command runs in a\n  throwaway directory with timeouts, capped outputs, and its whole process group is killed at the\n  end; `sandbox-exec` (macOS) or `bwrap` (Linux) is used when present. One GPU job at a time."
"Residual risk: **`exec` runs native code on machines that opted in.** The sandbox helpers are\nbest effort and absent on some systems. Enabling `exec` for a revision means trusting that code."
"- Each result is signed by the worker over `nuee/result/0 || lease || output_hash`; the output\n  hash is recomputed. A bad signature or hash bans the peer immediately.\n  *`nuee-proto::id::{sign_result, verify_result}`, `nuee-core::coordinator::on_result`.*\n- `Sample` mode: the coordinator recomputes a share of results itself and sends canary leases\n  with known answers, indistinguishable from normal ones. `Full` mode: two independent\n  executions. *`nuee-core::coordinator`, `nuee-core::verify`.*\n- Tolerance comparators for floating-point outputs (calibrated per job), exact hash comparison\n  otherwise. *`nuee-core::verify::Cmp`, comparators from `nuee-gpu` / `nuee-train`.*\n- A mismatch is arbitrated by the coordinator's own execution; the peer is banned only after that\n  confirmation, then every unverified result it produced is recomputed.\n  *`nuee-core::coordinator::ban`.*\n- `--approve` turns admission into a manual decision (`Msg::Pending`).\n- Training rejects aberrant deltas (trimmed mean, cosine and norm bounds in `TrainSpec`).\n  *`nuee-train` (in progress).*"
"Residual risk: **Sybil with ephemeral browser keys.** Identities are free, so bans only cost an\nattacker a reconnect, and in `Sample` mode a wrong result that is neither sampled nor a canary is\naccepted. The defence is the coordinator's recomputation: raise `sample_permille`, use `Full`,\nor use `--approve`. `None` mode trusts every worker."
"### 4.4 Fake coordinator, fingerprint forging"
"- The coordinator's public key is inside the link; QUIC/TLS 1.3 authenticates both ends with\n  their keys, so a worker only talks to that key. *iroh, `nuee-net::endpoint`.*\n- Manifests must be signed by that key (`nuee/manifest/0`) and name it as coordinator.\n  *`nuee-proto::manifest::SignedManifest::open`, called in `nuee-core::worker`.*\n- A 48-bit, six-emoji fingerprint derived only from the coordinator's key, shown on both sides\n  for comparison aloud. *`nuee-proto::fingerprint`.*"
"Residual risk: whoever controls the channel the link travels through can substitute their own\nlink; the fingerprint comparison is the check against that. 48 bits resist casual collisions,\nnot a determined offline search against a specific fingerprint over a long time."
"### 4.5 Intrusion and resource exhaustion"
"- Admission proof `HMAC-SHA256(secret, label || worker || coordinator)` compared in constant\n  time; it binds both identities so it cannot be replayed elsewhere.\n  *`nuee-proto::id::{proof, check_proof}`, `nuee-net::admit::Admission::verify`.*\n- `Hello` within 5 s, at most 64 handshakes pending, five wrong proofs in 60 s refuse the remote\n  for 60 s, throttling table bounded. *`nuee-net::admit`, `nuee-net::driver`.*\n- Bounded decoding everywhere: frame length checked before allocation, postcard decoding with a\n  size cap and no trailing bytes, every variable-length field a bounded container.\n  *`nuee-net::framing`, `nuee-proto::msg::decode_with_limit`, `nuee-proto::bounded`*; fuzzed by\n  `fuzz/fuzz_targets/decode_msg.rs`.\n- Peer, lease, job, asset and signalling caps (`MAX_PEERS`, `MAX_LEASES_PER_PEER`, `MAX_JOBS`,\n  `MAX_ASSETS_PER_JOB`, 64 WebRTC signals per peer per minute). *`nuee-proto::limits`,\n  `nuee-core::coordinator`.*\n- Blob access only for admitted peers, revoked on ban; iroh-blobs push disabled with a regression\n  test; per-peer blob budget; pinning quotas. *`nuee-net` `BlobHub::{admit, revoke}`,\n  `nuee-store` (in progress).*"
"Residual risk: a link holder is a member and can consume the share of resources the limits\nallow it. Volumetric attacks on the network are out of scope."
"Mitigations: iroh QUIC is end-to-end encrypted, including through relays; WebRTC data channels\nuse DTLS; shared files are encrypted with age before leaving the sharer, so pinners and relays\nonly see ciphertext. *iroh, `nuee-store::crypt` (in progress).*"
"Residual risk: **relay operators see IP addresses and connection metadata**; **WebRTC, when a\ncoordinator enables it, exposes members' IP addresses to each other** (ICE candidates)."
"Mitigations *(in progress: CLI and web asset fetching)*: asset URLs must be HTTPS, on an\nallowlisted host, and must not resolve to private, loopback or link-local addresses. `expose`\nonly forwards to its own loopback port."
"Mitigations *(in progress: `nuee-web`, `web/`, gateway)*: shared sites and service responses are\nnever rendered in the `maxgfr.github.io` origin. The console shows JSON; static previews use a\n`sandbox` iframe without permissions (an `srcdoc` iframe inherits the CSP anyway); the gateway\nsends `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox`; `nuee connect` serves on `localhost`."
"The page CSP allows inline `<style>` elements (`style-src-elem 'self' 'unsafe-inline'`) so the\npreview's inlined CSS applies, while `style-src-attr 'none'` keeps `style` attributes forbidden\n(the sanitizer strips them). This opens no injection path: no markup reaches the page except\nthrough DOM APIs (no `innerHTML`, Trusted Types enforced, scripts limited to `'self'`); the\npreview document runs in an opaque-origin sandbox with no rights; and image, font and connect\nsources stay restricted, so injected CSS cannot load from or send to arbitrary hosts."
"### 4.9 XSS and clickjacking in the web app"
"Mitigations *(in progress: `web/`)*: a `<meta>` CSP with `script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'`,\n`worker-src 'self'`, `connect-src https: wss: ws://localhost:* ws://127.0.0.1:*`,\n`object-src 'none'`, `base-uri 'none'`; Trusted Types with a single policy for `new Worker`; no\n`innerHTML` and no `style` attributes; the consent button refuses to render inside a frame; no\nCDN."
"Only the latest release receives security fixes. Older releases are not patched; upgrade to the\nlatest one."
"Use GitHub private vulnerability reporting on\n[maxgfr/nuee](https://github.com/maxgfr/nuee/security/advisories/new). Do not open a public issue\nfor a vulnerability."
"Please include the affected version or commit, what an attacker needs (a link? a running swarm?\na position on the network?), steps or a proof of concept, and the impact you observed."
"You can expect an acknowledgement within 72 hours. We will keep you informed while a fix is\nprepared, agree on a disclosure date with you, and credit you in the advisory unless you prefer\notherwise."
"- the `nuee` command-line tool and every crate in this repository;\n- the web application served from `https://maxgfr.github.io/nuee/` (sources in `web/` and\n  `crates/nuee-web`);\n- the wire protocol (`docs/PROTOCOL.md`): admission, signatures, links, manifests, bounded\n  decoding;\n- task isolation: a task module or an `exec` job escaping the limits described in\n  `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`;\n- the `nuee relay` server shipped with the tool."
"Out of scope, or accepted by design (see `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` for the reasoning):"
"- **Relay operators see IP addresses.** Traffic through an iroh relay is end-to-end encrypted,\n  but the relay sees which endpoints connect and, for browsers, their IP address. The n0 public\n  relays are meant for development and hobby use; run your own relay (`nuee relay`) for anything\n  else.\n- **Anyone holding a link is a member.** A job, file, site or service link is a bearer secret.\n  Sharing it grants what it says.\n- **A coordinator chooses what its workers run.** Joining a swarm means running that\n  coordinator's tasks inside the WebAssembly sandbox and limits. A malicious task that stays\n  inside those limits (burning its CPU budget, returning wrong answers) is not a vulnerability.\n- **`exec` runs native code.** It is disabled by default; a worker that enables it for a\n  repository and revision trusts that code with its machine, within the isolation listed in the\n  threat model.\n- **WebRTC exposes IP addresses** to other swarm members when the coordinator enables it.\n- Denial of service needing traffic volume comparable to the target's own bandwidth.\n- Vulnerabilities in dependencies that are not reachable from Nuée; report those upstream."
"Before each release the code goes through a security audit and a review of the pending changes.\nNo release ships while a finding rated high or critical is open."
"//! WGSL compute tasks: the input format, and (feature `native`) a wgpu runner.\n//!\n//! # Task format (`NWG1`)\n//!\n//! The module is the WGSL source (UTF-8, at most [`MAX_SOURCE`] bytes; its BLAKE3 is the\n//! module hash). A task input is, little-endian:\n//!\n//! ```text\n//! b\"NWG1\"\n//! u32 entry_len, entry point name (UTF-8, 1..=MAX_ENTRY bytes)\n//! u32 wx, u32 wy, u32 wz          workgroup counts, each 1..=65535\n//! u32 n                           bindings, 1..=8\n//! n times:\n//!   u32 binding                   binding number in @group(0), distinct\n//!   u32 kind                      0 uniform, 1 storage read-only, 2 storage read_write (output)\n//!   u32 size                      bytes, a non-zero multiple of 4, at most 64 MiB\n//!   size bytes of data            for kinds 0 and 1 only\n//! ```\n//!\n//! The sizes of all bindings add up to at most 128 MiB, at least one binding is an output and\n//! nothing follows the last binding. The output of a task is the final contents of its kind-2\n//! buffers, concatenated in the order they appear in the input. `web/app/worker/gpu.js`\n//! implemen"
"8; 4] = *b\"NWG1\";\n/// Largest WGSL source.\npub const MAX_SOURCE: usize = 1 << 20;\n/// Longest entry point name.\npub const MAX_ENTRY: usize = 256;\n/// Most bindings per task.\npub const MAX_BINDINGS: usize = 8;\n/// Largest workgroup count per dimension.\npub const MAX_WORKGROUPS: u32 = 65_535;\n/// Largest single binding.\npub const MAX_BINDING_SIZE: u32 = 64 << 20;\n/// Largest sum of all bindings.\npub const MAX_TOTAL_SIZE: u64 = 128 << 20;\n\n/// The WGSL twin of the mandelbrot task (`tasks/mandelbrot/mandelbrot.wgsl`).\npub const MANDELBROT_WGSL: &str = include_str!(\"../../../tasks/mandelbrot/mandelbrot.wgsl\");\n\n/// Comparator of WGSL mandelbrot jobs: f32 on GPUs drifts from the CPU's f64 near the set's\n/// boundary, so a few pixels may be off.\npub const MANDELBROT_COMPARE: nuee_proto::Comparator = nuee_proto::Comparator::ElementTolerance {\n    abs: 2.0,\n    rel: 0.0,\n    max_bad_permille: 20,\n};\n\n/// What a binding is used for.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum BindingKind {\n    /// `var<uniform>`, filled from the input.\n    Uniform,\n    /// `var<storage, read>`, fille"
"are part of the output.\n    Output,\n}\n\nimpl BindingKind {\n    fn from_u32(v: u32) -> Option<BindingKind> {\n        match v {\n            0 => Some(BindingKind::Uniform),\n            1 => Some(BindingKind::ReadOnly),\n            2 => Some(BindingKind::Output),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn code(self) -> u32 {\n        match self {\n            BindingKind::Uniform => 0,\n            BindingKind::ReadOnly => 1,\n            BindingKind::Output => 2,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// One binding of a task input.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub struct Binding<'a> {\n    /// Binding number in `@group(0)`.\n    pub binding: u32,\n    /// Use.\n    pub kind: BindingKind,\n    /// Size in bytes.\n    pub size: u32,\n    /// Initial contents (empty for outputs).\n    pub data: &'a [u8],\n}\n\n/// A parsed task input, borrowing the input bytes.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub struct TaskInput<'a> {\n    /// Entry point name.\n    pub entry: &'a str,\n    /// Workgroup counts.\n    pub workgroups: [u32; 3],\n    /// Bindings, in input order.\n    pub bindings: Vec<Binding<'a>>,\n}\n\nimpl T"
"bindings\n            .iter()\n            .filter(|b| b.kind == BindingKind::Output)\n            .map(|b| u64::from(b.size))\n            .sum()\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Reader<'a> {\n    rest: &'a [u8],\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Reader<'a> {\n    fn take(&mut self, n: usize, what: &str) -> Result<&'a [u8], GpuError> {\n        if self.rest.len() < n {\n            return Err(GpuError::invalid(format!(\"input truncated in {what}\")));\n        }\n        let (head, tail) = self.rest.split_at(n);\n        self.rest = tail;\n        Ok(head)\n    }\n\n    fn u32(&mut self, what: &str) -> Result<u32, GpuError> {\n        let b = self.take(4, what)?;\n        let mut a = [0u8; 4];\n        a.copy_from_slice(b);\n        Ok(u32::from_le_bytes(a))\n    }\n}\n\n/// Parses and checks a task input; nothing is allocated beyond the binding list.\npub fn parse_input(input: &[u8]) -> Result<TaskInput<'_>, GpuError> {\n    let mut r = Reader { rest: input };\n    if r.take(4, \"the magic\")? != MAGIC {\n        return Err(GpuError::invalid(\"not an NWG1 input\"));\n    }\n    let entry_len = r.u32(\"the entry length\")? as usize;\n    if entry_len == 0 ||"
"));\n    }\n    let entry = std::str::from_utf8(r.take(entry_len, \"the entry point name\")?)\n        .map_err(|_| GpuError::invalid(\"entry point name is not UTF-8\"))?;\n    let mut workgroups = [0u32; 3];\n    for w in &mut workgroups {\n        *w = r.u32(\"the workgroup counts\")?;\n        if *w == 0 || *w > MAX_WORKGROUPS {\n            return Err(GpuError::invalid(\"workgroup count out of range\"));\n        }\n    }\n    let n = r.u32(\"the binding count\")? as usize;\n    if n == 0 || n > MAX_BINDINGS {\n        return Err(GpuError::invalid(\"binding count out of range\"));\n    }\n    let mut bindings: Vec<Binding<'_>> = Vec::with_capacity(n);\n    let mut total = 0u64;\n    for _ in 0..n {\n        let binding = r.u32(\"a binding number\")?;\n        let kind = BindingKind::from_u32(r.u32(\"a binding kind\")?)\n            .ok_or_else(|| GpuError::invalid(\"unknown binding kind\"))?;\n        let size = r.u32(\"a binding size\")?;\n        if size == 0 || !size.is_multiple_of(4) || size > MAX_BINDING_SIZE {\n            return Err(GpuError::invalid(format!(\n                \"binding {binding}: size must be a non-z"
"ing == binding) {\n            return Err(GpuError::invalid(format!(\n                \"binding {binding} declared twice\"\n            )));\n        }\n        total += u64::from(size);\n        if total > MAX_TOTAL_SIZE {\n            return Err(GpuError::invalid(\"bindings above 128 MiB in total\"));\n        }\n        let data = match kind {\n            BindingKind::Output => &[][..],\n            _ => r.take(size as usize, \"binding data\")?,\n        };\n        bindings.push(Binding {\n            binding,\n            kind,\n            size,\n            data,\n        });\n    }\n    if !r.rest.is_empty() {\n        return Err(GpuError::invalid(\"trailing bytes after the last binding\"));\n    }\n    if !bindings.iter().any(|b| b.kind == BindingKind::Output) {\n        return Err(GpuError::invalid(\"no output binding\"));\n    }\n    Ok(TaskInput {\n        entry,\n        workgroups,\n        bindings,\n    })\n}\n\n/// A binding to encode with [`encode_input`].\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\npub enum BindingSpec<'a> {\n    /// A uniform buffer with these contents.\n    Uniform(u32, &'a [u8]),\n    /// A read-only st"
"age buffer of this size.\n    Output(u32, u32),\n}\n\n/// Encodes a task input. Checks nothing: [`parse_input`] does on the other side.\npub fn encode_input(entry: &str, workgroups: [u32; 3], bindings: &[BindingSpec<'_>]) -> Vec<u8> {\n    let mut v = Vec::new();\n    let put = |v: &mut Vec<u8>, x: u32| v.extend_from_slice(&x.to_le_bytes());\n    v.extend_from_slice(&MAGIC);\n    put(&mut v, entry.len() as u32);\n    v.extend_from_slice(entry.as_bytes());\n    for w in workgroups {\n        put(&mut v, w);\n    }\n    put(&mut v, bindings.len() as u32);\n    for b in bindings {\n        match *b {\n            BindingSpec::Uniform(n, data) | BindingSpec::ReadOnly(n, data) => {\n                let kind = if matches!(b, BindingSpec::Uniform(..)) {\n                    BindingKind::Uniform\n                } else {\n                    BindingKind::ReadOnly\n                };\n                put(&mut v, n);\n                put(&mut v, kind.code());\n                put(&mut v, data.len() as u32);\n                v.extend_from_slice(data);\n            }\n            BindingSpec::Output(n, size) => {"
"&mut v, size);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    v\n}\n\n/// Input of a [`MANDELBROT_WGSL`] task: a `width` x `height` tile from (`x0`, `y0`), `step`\n/// apart, at most `max_iter` iterations.\npub fn mandelbrot_input(\n    x0: f32,\n    y0: f32,\n    step: f32,\n    width: u32,\n    height: u32,\n    max_iter: u32,\n) -> Vec<u8> {\n    let mut params = Vec::with_capacity(24);\n    for f in [x0, y0, step] {\n        params.extend_from_slice(&f.to_le_bytes());\n    }\n    for u in [width, height, max_iter] {\n        params.extend_from_slice(&u.to_le_bytes());\n    }\n    encode_input(\n        \"main\",\n        [width.div_ceil(8), height.div_ceil(8), 1],\n        &[\n            BindingSpec::Uniform(0, &params),\n            BindingSpec::Output(1, width.saturating_mul(height).saturating_mul(4)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\n\n#[cfg(feature = \"native\")]\npub use native::{GpuRunner, adapter_caps, check_shader};\n\n#[cfg(feature = \"native\")]\nmod native {\n    use std::future::Future;\n    use std::pin::pin;\n    use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, AtomicUsize, Ordering};\n    use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\n    use std::task::{Conte"
"// @ts-check\n// The task worker pool. Each worker runs one lease at a time on a fresh instance; the pool\n// enforces deadlines by terminating the worker and starting a fresh one.\n/** @typedef {import('../api').TaskRequest} TaskRequest */\n/** @typedef {import('../api').TaskOutcome} TaskOutcome */\n/** @typedef {import('../api').TaskRun} TaskRun */\n/** @typedef {import('../api').TaskKind} TaskKind */\n/** @typedef {import('../api').Executor} Executor */\n/** @typedef {import('../api').FailKind} FailKind */\n\nexport const MiB = 2 ** 20;\nexport const GiB = 2 ** 30;\n/** Total memory all task instances may claim together. */\nexport const BUDGET_MOBILE = GiB;\nexport const BUDGET_DESKTOP = 4 * GiB;\n\nconst FAIL_KINDS = new Set(['trap', 'oom', 'timeout', 'stack_overflow', 'invalid', 'unsupported', 'cancelled', 'io']);\n\n/**\n * @typedef {{ cores: number, mobile: boolean, deviceMemoryGb?: number }} Device\n */\n\n/** Memory budget for all task instances together. @param {Device} d */\nexport function memoryBudget(d) {\n  let budget = d.mobile ? BUDGET_MOBILE : BUDGET_DESKTOP;\n  // navigator.deviceMemory i"
", (d.deviceMemoryGb * GiB) / 2);\n  return Math.max(budget, 256 * MiB);\n}\n\n/**\n * How many leases may run at once.\n * @param {Device & { wanted: number, maxMemory: number }} o\n */\nexport function poolSize(o) {\n  const cores = Math.max(1, Math.floor(o.cores) || 1);\n  const wanted = Math.min(cores, Math.max(0, Math.floor(o.wanted) || 0));\n  if (wanted === 0) return 0;\n  const per = Math.max(o.maxMemory, 16 * MiB);\n  const byMemory = Math.max(1, Math.floor(memoryBudget(o) / per));\n  return Math.min(wanted, byMemory);\n}\n\n/** Half the cores, rounded up: the default of the contribution slider. @param {number} cores */\nexport function defaultCores(cores) {\n  return Math.max(1, Math.ceil(Math.max(1, cores) / 2));\n}\n\n/**\n * Memory offered to a swarm, in MiB, for `slots` concurrent leases.\n * @param {Device} d\n * @param {number} slots\n */\nexport function offeredMemMb(d, slots) {\n  const per = Math.floor(memoryBudget(d) / Math.max(1, slots) / MiB);\n  return Math.max(64, Math.min(1024, per));\n}\n\n/**\n * The part of a Worker the pool uses (a real Worker, or a fake in tests).\n * @typedef {{\n *   pos"
": { data: unknown }) => void) | null,\n *   onerror: ((e: { message?: string }) => void) | null,\n * }} TaskWorkerLike\n */\n\n/**\n * @typedef {{ w: TaskWorkerLike, busy: boolean, modules: Set<string>, abort?: (o: TaskOutcome) => void }} Slot\n * @typedef {{ req: TaskRequest, resolve: (o: TaskOutcome) => void }} Job\n */\n\n/** @param {FailKind} kind @param {string} detail @returns {TaskOutcome} */\nexport function fail(kind, detail) {\n  return { ok: false, kind, detail };\n}\n\n/** @param {unknown} v @returns {TaskOutcome} */\nfunction checkOutcome(v) {\n  if (typeof v === 'object' && v !== null) {\n    const o = /** @type {Record<string, unknown>} */ (v);\n    if (o.ok === true && o.output instanceof Uint8Array) return { ok: true, output: o.output };\n    if (o.ok === false && typeof o.kind === 'string' && FAIL_KINDS.has(o.kind)) {\n      return fail(/** @type {FailKind} */ (o.kind), typeof o.detail === 'string' ? o.detail : '');\n    }\n  }\n  return fail('io', 'malformed reply from a task worker');\n}\n\n/** Whether a view owns its whole buffer, so it can be transferred. @param {Uint8Array} u */\nexport f"
"nstanceof ArrayBuffer;\n}\n\nexport class Pool {\n  /**\n   * @param {{ create: () => TaskWorkerLike, device: Device, slots: number }} o\n   */\n  constructor(o) {\n    this.create = o.create;\n    this.device = o.device;\n    this.slots = o.slots;\n    /** @type {Slot[]} */\n    this.workers = [];\n    /** @type {Job[]} */\n    this.queue = [];\n    /** Module bytes by hash, as first received; sent to workers that lack them. */\n    /** @type {Map<string, Uint8Array>} */\n    this.modules = new Map();\n    this.running = 0;\n    this.stopped = false;\n  }\n\n  /** @param {number} n concurrent leases (0 holds new leases in the queue) */\n  setSlots(n) {\n    this.slots = Math.max(0, Math.floor(n));\n    this.trim();\n    this.pump();\n  }\n\n  /** @param {number} maxMemory */\n  limit(maxMemory) {\n    return poolSize({ ...this.device, wanted: this.slots, maxMemory });\n  }\n\n  /** @param {TaskRequest} req @returns {Promise<TaskOutcome>} */\n  run(req) {\n    if (this.stopped) return Promise.resolve(fail('cancelled', 'the pool is stopped'));\n    if (req.moduleHash && req.module && !this.modules.has(req.moduleHash)) th"
"ush({ req, resolve });\n      this.pump();\n    });\n  }\n\n  pump() {\n    while (this.queue.length > 0 && !this.stopped) {\n      const head = this.queue[0];\n      if (!head || this.running >= this.limit(head.req.maxMemory)) return;\n      this.queue.shift();\n      this.dispatch(head);\n    }\n  }\n\n  /** Drops idle workers beyond the slot count. */\n  trim() {\n    const keep = Math.max(this.slots, this.running);\n    for (let i = this.workers.length - 1; i >= 0 && this.workers.length > keep; i -= 1) {\n      const s = this.workers[i];\n      if (s && !s.busy) {\n        s.w.terminate();\n        this.workers.splice(i, 1);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  /** @returns {Slot} */\n  idle() {\n    const free = this.workers.find((s) => !s.busy);\n    if (free) return free;\n    const s = { w: this.create(), busy: false, modules: new Set() };\n    this.workers.push(s);\n    return s;\n  }\n\n  /** @param {Slot} s */\n  drop(s) {\n    s.w.onmessage = null;\n    s.w.onerror = null;\n    s.w.terminate();\n    const i = this.workers.indexOf(s);\n    if (i !== -1) this.workers.splice(i, 1);\n  }\n\n  /** @param {Job} job */\n  dispatch(jo"
"ules.get(req.moduleHash);\n      if (!known) {\n        job.resolve(fail('invalid', `module ${req.moduleHash} was never received`));\n        return;\n      }\n      module = known;\n    }\n    const s = this.idle();\n    s.busy = true;\n    this.running += 1;\n    let settled = false;\n    /** @param {TaskOutcome} out @param {boolean} replace */\n    const finish = (out, replace) => {\n      if (settled) return;\n      settled = true;\n      clearTimeout(timer);\n      this.running -= 1;\n      if (replace) this.drop(s);\n      else s.busy = false;\n      job.resolve(out);\n      this.trim();\n      this.pump();\n    };\n    const timer = setTimeout(() => finish(fail('timeout', `deadline of ${req.deadlineMs} ms exceeded`), true), req.deadlineMs);\n    s.w.onmessage = (e) => {\n      if (req.moduleHash) s.modules.add(req.moduleHash);\n      finish(checkOutcome(e.data), false);\n    };\n    s.w.onerror = (e) => finish(fail('io', e.message ?? 'task worker error'), true);\n    s.abort = (o) => finish(o, true);\n    /** @type {TaskRun} */\n    const msg = {\n      op: 'run',\n      lease: req.lease,\n      kind: req.kind"
"uleHash) ? module.slice() : undefined,\n      input: req.input,\n      deadlineMs: req.deadlineMs,\n      maxMemory: req.maxMemory,\n    };\n    /** @type {Transferable[]} */\n    const transfer = [];\n    if (owned(req.input)) transfer.push(req.input.buffer);\n    if (msg.module) transfer.push(msg.module.buffer);\n    s.w.postMessage(msg, transfer);\n  }\n\n  stop() {\n    this.stopped = true;\n    for (const job of this.queue.splice(0)) job.resolve(fail('cancelled', 'stopped'));\n    for (const s of [...this.workers]) {\n      if (s.busy && s.abort) s.abort(fail('cancelled', 'stopped'));\n      else this.drop(s);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/** Executors for kinds other than plain wasm (S-018 wgsl, S-019 embed, S-020 llm register here). */\n/** @type {Map<TaskKind, Executor>} */\nexport const kindExecutors = new Map();\n\n/** @param {TaskKind} kind @param {Executor} ex */\nexport function registerExecutor(kind, ex) {\n  kindExecutors.set(kind, ex);\n}\n\n/** Kinds this page can run. @returns {TaskKind[]} */\nexport function supportedKinds() {\n  return ['wasm', 'trainWasm', ...[...kindExecutors.keys()].filter((k) => k !== '"
"eases (same task ABI) go to the\n * pool, other kinds to their registered executor.\n * @param {Pool} pool\n * @param {Map<TaskKind, Executor>} [extra]\n * @returns {Executor}\n */\nexport function makeExecutor(pool, extra = kindExecutors) {\n  return (task) => {\n    const ex = extra.get(task.kind);\n    if (ex) return ex(task);\n    if (task.kind === 'wasm' || task.kind === 'trainWasm') return pool.run(task);\n    return Promise.resolve(fail('unsupported', `no executor for ${task.kind} in this page`));\n  };\n}"
"//! Shareable links: `<base>#/<K>/<PAYLOAD>` where PAYLOAD is uppercase base32 (no padding) of\n//! `version || postcard(body) || first 4 bytes of blake3(version || body)`.\n\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize, de::DeserializeOwned};\n\nuse crate::bounded::{BoundedString, BoundedVec};\nuse crate::id::{EndpointId, Hash};\nuse crate::limits::MAX_LINK_PROVIDERS;\nuse crate::msg::{Provider, decode_with_limit, encode_with_limit};\n\n/// Base URL used when none is configured.\npub const DEFAULT_BASE: &str = \"https://maxgfr.github.io/nuee/\";\n/// Current link format version.\npub const LINK_VERSION: u8 = 1;\nconst CHECKSUM_LEN: usize = 4;\n/// Largest decoded link body.\npub const MAX_LINK_BODY: usize = 4096;\n\n/// Invite to a compute swarm (`J`).\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct JobLink {\n    /// Coordinator identity.\n    pub coordinator: EndpointId,\n    /// Coordinator relay URL.\n    pub relay: BoundedString<256>,\n    /// Admission secret.\n    pub secret: [u8; 16],\n}\n\n/// Shared file or site (`F`, `S`).\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)"
", MAX_LINK_PROVIDERS>,\n    /// Ciphertext hash.\n    pub hash: Hash,\n    /// Ciphertext size.\n    pub size: u64,\n    /// Access token presented to providers.\n    pub access: [u8; 16],\n    /// age X25519 identity that decrypts the content.\n    pub key: [u8; 32],\n}\n\n/// File link without the decryption key, given to pinners (`P`).\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct PinLink {\n    /// Peers serving the ciphertext.\n    pub providers: BoundedVec<Provider, MAX_LINK_PROVIDERS>,\n    /// Ciphertext hash.\n    pub hash: Hash,\n    /// Ciphertext size.\n    pub size: u64,\n    /// Access token presented to providers.\n    pub access: [u8; 16],\n}\n\n/// Exposed service (`X`).\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct ServiceLink {\n    /// Host identity.\n    pub host: EndpointId,\n    /// Host relay URL.\n    pub relay: BoundedString<256>,\n    /// Admission secret.\n    pub secret: [u8; 16],\n}\n\n/// Any link a user can paste.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Link {\n    /// Compute swarm.\n    Job(JobLink),\n    /// Shared file"
"te(FileLink),\n}\n\n/// A link cannot be read.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, thiserror::Error)]\npub enum LinkError {\n    /// Incomplete: too short, bad base32 or checksum mismatch.\n    #[error(\"the link looks truncated; copy it again in full\")]\n    Truncated,\n    /// Made by a newer or unknown version.\n    #[error(\"link version {0} is not supported; update nuee\")]\n    UnsupportedVersion(u8),\n    /// Checksum is fine but the content is invalid.\n    #[error(\"the link is corrupt\")]\n    Corrupt,\n    /// Valid link of another kind than expected.\n    #[error(\"this is a different kind of link\")]\n    WrongKind,\n}\n\nimpl FileLink {\n    /// Same link without the decryption key.\n    pub fn pin_link(&self) -> PinLink {\n        PinLink {\n            providers: self.providers.clone(),\n            hash: self.hash,\n            size: self.size,\n            access: self.access,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn encode_payload<T: Serialize>(body: &T) -> String {\n    let mut bytes = vec![LINK_VERSION];\n    match encode_with_limit(body, MAX_LINK_BODY) {\n        Ok(b) => bytes.extend_from_slice(&b),"
"e3::hash(&bytes);\n    bytes.extend_from_slice(&sum.as_bytes()[..CHECKSUM_LEN]);\n    data_encoding::BASE32_NOPAD.encode(&bytes)\n}\n\nfn join(base: &str, kind: char, payload: &str) -> String {\n    let base = base.split('#').next().unwrap_or(base);\n    format!(\"{base}#/{kind}/{payload}\")\n}\n\n/// Splits user input into its kind letter and payload.\nfn split(input: &str) -> Result<(char, &str), LinkError> {\n    let s = input.trim();\n    let s = match s.rfind('#') {\n        Some(i) => &s[i + 1..],\n        None => s,\n    };\n    let s = s.trim_start_matches('/');\n    let mut chars = s.chars();\n    let kind = chars.next().ok_or(LinkError::Truncated)?;\n    let rest = chars.as_str();\n    if rest.is_empty() {\n        return Err(LinkError::Truncated);\n    }\n    let payload = rest.strip_prefix('/').ok_or(LinkError::Corrupt)?;\n    Ok((kind.to_ascii_uppercase(), payload.trim()))\n}\n\nfn decode_payload<T: DeserializeOwned>(payload: &str) -> Result<T, LinkError> {\n    let upper = payload.to_ascii_uppercase();\n    let bytes = data_encoding::BASE32_NOPAD\n        .decode(upper.as_bytes())\n        .map_err(|_|"
"if version != LINK_VERSION {\n        return Err(LinkError::UnsupportedVersion(version));\n    }\n    if bytes.len() < 1 + CHECKSUM_LEN {\n        return Err(LinkError::Truncated);\n    }\n    let (data, sum) = bytes.split_at(bytes.len() - CHECKSUM_LEN);\n    if blake3::hash(data).as_bytes()[..CHECKSUM_LEN] != *sum {\n        return Err(LinkError::Truncated);\n    }\n    decode_with_limit(&data[1..], MAX_LINK_BODY).map_err(|_| LinkError::Corrupt)\n}\n\nimpl Link {\n    /// Kind letter used in the URL.\n    pub fn kind_letter(&self) -> char {\n        match self {\n            Link::Job(_) => 'J',\n            Link::File(_) => 'F',\n            Link::Service(_) => 'X',\n            Link::Site(_) => 'S',\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Renders the link under `base` (for example [`DEFAULT_BASE`]).\n    pub fn to_url(&self, base: &str) -> String {\n        let payload = match self {\n            Link::Job(l) => encode_payload(l),\n            Link::File(l) | Link::Site(l) => encode_payload(l),\n            Link::Service(l) => encode_payload(l),\n        };\n        join(base, self.kind_letter(), &payload)\n    }\n\n    //"
"ror> {\n        let (kind, payload) = split(input)?;\n        match kind {\n            'J' => decode_payload(payload).map(Link::Job),\n            'F' => decode_payload(payload).map(Link::File),\n            'X' => decode_payload(payload).map(Link::Service),\n            'S' => decode_payload(payload).map(Link::Site),\n            'P' => Err(LinkError::WrongKind),\n            _ => Err(LinkError::Corrupt),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl PinLink {\n    /// Renders the pin link under `base`.\n    pub fn to_url(&self, base: &str) -> String {\n        join(base, 'P', &encode_payload(self))\n    }\n\n    /// Parses a pin link; other link kinds give [`LinkError::WrongKind`].\n    pub fn parse(input: &str) -> Result<PinLink, LinkError> {\n        let (kind, payload) = split(input)?;\n        match kind {\n            'P' => decode_payload(payload),\n            'J' | 'F' | 'X' | 'S' => Err(LinkError::WrongKind),\n            _ => Err(LinkError::Corrupt),\n        }\n    }\n}"
